√labh is ātmanepada (labhate). Perfect ātmanepada 2nd plural: -dhve ending → lebhidhve. Root labh: perfect stem labh- doubled: la-labh-; with ā-... Actually the rule: the root with medial a between single consonants undergoes e-substitution and loss of reduplication (a→e, abhyāsalopa) in the perfect when the root has a medial a with initial and final consonants, in the weak forms (kit/ṅit). So labh → lebh- (like tepa, from tap). But labh is a special case: "labh" in liṭ: lebhe, lebhāte, lebhire. Yes, since liṭ ātmanepada is ṅit, no guṇa; the ātmanepada endings are treated as kit by the rule "liṭ ca ... asaṃyogāt liṭ kit" . Fine; and rule 6.4.120 requires the root to have a ... "thali ca seṭi" and "ato ekahalmadhye anādeśādeḥ liṭi kṅiti" — works for labh. Labh has initial l and final bh... fine, so lebh-.

