√śak (svādi, śaknoti, parasmaipada) perfect: śaśāka, śekatuḥ, śekuḥ; 1st plural: śekima.

Reduplication: śak → śa-śak. Before the vit endings (atus, us, va, ma, etc.) the root has medial a between single consonants, so by "thali ca seṭi" / "atra lopo 'bhyāsasya" it gets the ē-substitution (e-ttva-abhyāsalopa). Condition: root with medial a, single consonant between, preceded by a non-reduplicative... The reduplicated syllable has the same consonant as the following one only if the initial is non-ca-class and so on. Here the ē-substitution applies to roots with a-vowel, an initial consonant not subject to change by the reduplication, and ending in a single consonant. For śak: śekatuḥ, śekuḥ, śekima. The ending ma is liṭ 1st plural parasmaipada "ima" after the augment iṭ: śekima. The ē-substitution requires the ending be kit/ṅit. Liṭ is ṅit except for the pit endings (ṇal, thal, ṇal). Ma is ṅit by "asaṃyogāl liṭ kit". Śak has a single consonant after a, so the ē-substitution applies. Since śak is not seṭ, it gets no iṭ in general, but with the rule "kṛsṛbhṛvṛstu..." śak is anit; hence śekima? Actually the form is śekima, which is a standard form. Not "śaśakma"? In the dhātupāṭha, śak is anit (śaknoti), so 1st plural would be śekima in the paradigm: śeka, śekatuḥ, śekuḥ; śakitha/śaktha, śekathuḥ, śeka; śaśāka/śaśaka, śekiva, śekima. Yes, the standard paradigm gives śekima.

